      "text": "A ”Officer of (the) Law” or ”Law Officer” is either a Trustee or Agent, duly authorised in relation to the administration and enforcement of the law. Whether a Trustee or Agent, a Law Officer is duty bound to the fiduciary and moral obligations of good faith, good character and good conduct, above and beyond any affiliation, membership or oath to some other guild, association or interest.\n\nMoral Duty of Law Officer to the Law\nWhile certain modern societies and jurisdictions have spent considerable time creating complex and convoluted systems of delegation whereby only one (or sometimes two) person(s) may be “legally” regarded as a Law Officer, with everyone else being his/her agent – such semantic games are meaningless when it comes to the fundamental moral duty of anyone acting – in any capacity as an officer (or agent) of the law.\n\nA Law Officer since the beginning of human civilisation is someone entrusted by sacred oath and promise to protect, uphold and administer the law – without fear or favour. Regardless of infantile arguments of legal status, a Law Officer is duty bound to function to the highest fiduciary and moral standards of good faith, good character and good conduct. Anything less is to fundamental undermine the integrity of the rule of law and the survival of a society itself.\n\nLaw Officers cannot serve two masters\nIn ancient systems of law, Law Officers were forbidden to belong to fraternities, guilds or business that would otherwise cause them to come in conflict with their fiduciary and moral obligations to the law. Such respect to the integrity of the law and rule of law was “thrown out” in many jurisdictions nearly two centuries ago, when private fraternities and guild were permitted to act “as a whole” as if the public entity. Thus public courts became private and the true nature of many courts and their functions in some of the most advanced economies in the world have been deliberately clouded ever since.\n\nMorally and spiritually, a Law Officer cannot server “two masters” of law. It is an abomination that people have ever been permitted to act in the capacity of an officer of the court or a law enforcement officer whilst being a “card carrying member” of a private fraternity with its own oaths, loyalties, pledges and interests. This in no way is an imputation on individuals who serve in such functions, or even the private bodies themselves. It is simply a fact of reason and a moral imperative that officers of the law are “above reproach”. Such a position is both impossible to claim and to effect if an individual is twice pledged to two different “masters” of law being the state and his/her guild.\n\nLaw Officers cannot serve under letters of marque\nDuring the 17th Century proxy wars that engulfed European colonies in the Americas, Africa and Asia, certain governments concocted an abomination and absurdity of law by effectively claiming they could “legalize piracy” through the use of “letters of marque and reprisal” and the position of “privateer”. Nowhere was this wicked and morally repugnant corruption against proper law used more than in the American colonies.\n\nSince the 19th century, there is overwhelming and incontrovertable evidence that the practice of issuing immoral, absurd and wicked “letters of marque and reprisal” has continued within the ranks of claimed “officers of the law” – tantamount to granting immunity from prosecution for cruel, evil and criminal behaviour.\n\nGiven a proper Officer of Law is duty bound to their fiduciary and moral obligations to uphold and serve the rule of law, the use of such devices as “letters of marque” are wholly contradictory and represent categoric proof of a fundamental breach of trust and betrayal of the foundations of society and the law itself.",
